{
  "id": "hashmap-two-sum-code",
  "title": "Two sum with a seen-map",
  "kind": "code",
  "topic": "hashmap",
  "difficulty": "easy",
  "est_minutes": 5,
  "prompt": "Implement twoSum(nums []int, target int) []int returning the two indices whose values add to target. Exactly one answer exists. One pass, O(n).",
  "code": {
    "stub": "func twoSum(nums []int, target int) []int {\n\t// your code here\n\treturn nil\n}",
    "tests": "import (\n\t\"sort\"\n\t\"testing\"\n)\n\nfunc TestTwoSum(t *testing.T) {\n\tcases := []struct {\n\t\tnums   []int\n\t\ttarget int\n\t\twant   []int\n\t}{\n\t\t{[]int{2, 7, 11, 15}, 9, []int{0, 1}},\n\t\t{[]int{3, 2, 4}, 6, []int{1, 2}},\n\t\t{[]int{3, 3}, 6, []int{0, 1}},\n\t\t{[]int{-1, -2, -3, -4, -5}, -8, []int{2, 4}},\n\t}\n\tfor _, c := range cases {\n\t\tgot := twoSum(c.nums, c.target)\n\t\tif len(got) != 2 {\n\t\t\tt.Fatalf(\"twoSum(%v, %d) = %v, want two indices\", c.nums, c.target, got)\n\t\t}\n\t\tsort.Ints(got)\n\t\tif got[0] != c.want[0] || got[1] != c.want[1] {\n\t\t\tt.Fatalf(\"twoSum(%v, %d) = %v, want %v\", c.nums, c.target, got, c.want)\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}"
  },
  "answer": "func twoSum(nums []int, target int) []int {\n\tseen := make(map[int]int, len(nums))\n\tfor i, v := range nums {\n\t\tif j, ok := seen[target-v]; ok {\n\t\t\treturn []int{j, i}\n\t\t}\n\t\tseen[v] = i\n\t}\n\treturn nil\n}",
  "explanation": "Store value -> index as you go and look for the complement before inserting. Checking first is what stops an element pairing with itself, and it means you never need a second pass.",
  "hints": [
    "One map, filled as you go. What do you look up before you insert?",
    "For nums[i] you want target-nums[i]. Store value -> index."
  ],
  "refs": [
    "LC 1 Two Sum"
  ]
}
